    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d667">

                <pb facs="0792" n="680" xml:id="pg680"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/12982: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Canton</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Spiker</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Canton</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-03-04T12:00:00+08:00">March 4,
                        1935—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received March 6—6:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Referring to my telegram of March 3, 6 p.m.,<anchor n="89" xml:id="d667-f1"/>
                    telegram from Kweiyang reports Tsunyi now taken by Communists, combined Northern
                    and Kweichow forces falling back to take defensive positions on south bank of Wu
                    River, after disastrous defeat of five brigades between Tsunyi and Meitan.
                    Government leaders in Kweichow broadcasting appeals for immediate reenforcements
                    to enable them to hold present positions. Kweiyang defences being strengthened.
                    I have again telegraphed American missionaries in that city and Kiensi to
                    exercise every precaution. Sent to the Department, repeated to the Legation,
                    Nanking and Hankow as well as my March 3, 6 p.m.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Spiker</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
